## Why This Is Asked
Privacy-preserving on-device experiments enable rapid iteration without raw data leaving devices, aligning with regulatory scrutiny and low-latency user experience goals.

## Key Concepts
- On-device flag evaluation and cohort management
- Local storage and memory budgeting
- Delta-based telemetry with minimal data exposure
- Safe rollout, rollback, and veto mechanisms
- Compliance and auditability considerations

## Code Example
```javascript
// Local flag evaluation snippet (pseudo)
function evalFlag(user, flagRepo) {
  const cohort = user.cohortId;
  const flag = flagRepo.getFlag(cohort);
  return flag.evaluate(user);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question assesses a candidate's ability to translate product goals into a financially grounded technical plan, balancing on-device ML costs, user privacy, and rollout risk.

## Key Concepts

- On-device ML cost modeling (energy, memory, inference latency)
- Data transfer savings and network impact
- Privacy controls and regulatory compliance
- OTA update cadence and maintenance costs
- Rollout strategy, drift monitoring, and go/no-go criteria

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-cost model for CBA calculations
function cba(params) {
  const energy = params.inferences * params.energyPerInference;
  const memory = params.modelSize + params.cacheSize;
  const dataSavings = params.uploadSize * params.reductionRate;
  return { energy, memory, dataSavings };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to model cross-device costs, trade-offs, and governance for a live feature-flag infrastructure. Tests data locality, privacy, offline behavior, and rollout risk at scale.

## Key Concepts

- Local evaluation cost vs backend traffic
- Data minimization and privacy/compliance
- Rollout strategy and drift management
- Maintenance and rollback costs

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple CBA calculator (illustrative)
function cba(months, backendCost, localKB, energyPerEval) {
  const backendSavings = backendCost * months;
  const localCosts = localKB * 0.002 + energyPerEval * months;
  return backendSavings - localCosts;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle offline devices during experiments?
- What telemetry would you collect to justify cost?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a privacy-preserving, on-device ML feature with a clear financial rationale and real-world constraints.

## Key Concepts

- On-device ML size/latency trade-offs
- Energy, memory, and storage budgeting for mobile apps
- Telemetry costs and delta-based model updates
- Regulatory/compliance costs and risk assessment
- Go/no-go criteria and break-even planning

## Code Example

```javascript
function inferLeadScore(features, model) {
  // simple LR-like dot product
  let score = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < features.length; i++) {
    score += features[i] * model.weights[i];
  }
  return 1 / (1 + Math.exp(-score));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate drift and schedule model updates offline?
- How would you audit privacy, regulatory compliance, and data minimization during rollout?

## Why This Question Is Asked
This evaluates cost modeling for federated, multi-app ML with privacy constraints—a realistic production use case at companies like Google (Gboard), Apple, and Meta. It tests ability to identify cost drivers, trade-offs between privacy and accuracy, and phased rollout planning under regulatory constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Federated learning with secure aggregation and differential privacy
- Cross-app governance, regional data locality (GDPR, CCPA, PIPL)
- Bandwidth, energy, and memory budgeting on mobile devices
- Model drift detection, concept drift, and maintenance pipelines
- Phased regional rollout with go/no-go gates

## Code Example
```javascript
function calculateCBAPhase(devices, roundsPerDay, daysPerMonth, payloadKB, costPerGB) {
  const activeDevices = devices * 0.30;
  const monthlyUplinkGB = activeDevices * roundsPerDay * daysPerMonth * payloadKB / 1024 / 1024;
  const monthlyCost = monthlyUplinkGB * costPerGB;
  return {
    devices, activeDevices, monthlyUplinkGB, monthlyCost,
    breakEvenUsers: monthlyCost / 0.10
  };
}

function rolloutPhase(phase, criteria) {
  return {
    phase,
    go: criteria.every(c => c.passed),
    gates: criteria
  };
}
```

